{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-330642",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/12f35be1eac4ea4550df9e6e22f6650bcd9d532943902ad8e6239a6b8c6587f9.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>-a)=P(Z<a)=\u03a6(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z>-0.74).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/9608411f431d6a72af9185ce77b723c7145b1d900559ad9f78d125e92416fbe8.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z<0.74).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/49e48de25c1183b24176fbfca868c6d07b8367632ad63c158181e3538222fc3d.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z>-0.74)=P(Z<0.74)=\u03a6(0.74)=0.7704.\n$$",
